The filing explains the change in the consolidated gross profit rate for two years. The rate rose 8 basis points in fiscal 2026 and 40 basis points in fiscal 2025. [1]

The fiscal 2026 increase was driven mainly by the Walmart U.S. segment, thanks to disciplined inventory management, along with growth in higher margin businesses globally. [1] The fiscal 2025 increase was also driven mainly by Walmart U.S., in that case from managing prices in line with its competitive historic price gaps, plus growth in higher margin businesses globally. [1] In both years, mix shifts into lower margin merchandise categories across segments, and ongoing channel and format mix shifts in Walmart International, partially offset the increases. [1]

By segment, Walmart International moved differently. Its gross profit rate fell 49 basis points in fiscal 2026, mainly because of channel and format mix shifts and strategic investments in price and delivery capabilities, partly offset by higher margin businesses. It rose 20 basis points in fiscal 2025 on improved eCommerce margin and business mix changes, partly offset by channel and format mix changes. [2]

The filing also notes that pricing and merchandising strategies, such as absorbing cost increases, reducing prices in certain categories, and emphasizing private label brands, have affected and may continue to affect gross profit as a percentage of net sales. [3]